BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Darwin’s solution: 17 years of natural 
 selection on Oahu (research hook)
DTSTART:20260907T150000Z
DTEND:20260907T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260906T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-ongoing-darwinssolution-20260907@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot. Carried over
  unchanged from the Aug 30 2026 run.\n\nExpert-authorship check: PARTIAL M
 ATCH. "Darwin's solution: honeybees survive mite vectors and viruses throu
 gh natural selection" (Neumann\, Beaurepaire\, Bouga\, Brodschneider\, Car
 reck\, Dahle\, de Miranda\, de Sousa\, Dietemann\, Eliash\, Ellis\, Erler
 \, Evans\, Locke\, Martin\, Mondet\, Morawetz\, Moro\, Özkırım\, Panzie
 ra\, Traynor and others — a ~20-author COLOSS-network consortium piece) 
 lists James D. Ellis as a co-author — almost certainly the same "Jamie E
 llis (U Florida)" already in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md's Batch 1 university o
 utreach list (not yet contacted). Worth a soft mention if outreach to him 
 ever happens\, though he's one of ~20 co-authors on an opinion piece\, not
  the lead — don't overstate his role. No new name worth adding to KEY_PE
 OPLE_CONTACTS.md\; the rest of the author list is European/international C
 OLOSS-network researchers outside that file's US-only scope.\n\nPost text:
 \nNew research just backed up exactly what SaveTheHives is built to track.
  A 17-year study of a free-living honey bee population on Oahu found that 
 colonies left alone with Varroa mites — no treatment\, no rescue — cra
 shed hard at first\, then rebounded\, evolving better mite-detection and r
 emoval behavior along the way. Natural selection did in under two decades 
 what chemical treatments still can't fully solve.\n\nThe paper (Trends in 
 Parasitology\, out this month) has nearly 20 co-authors from the global be
 e-research community — including Jamie Ellis at the University of Florid
 a\, already on our own list of researchers to reach out to. Its takeaway: 
 the field needs standardized\, ongoing monitoring of colony survival to fi
 nd more populations like this one. That's exactly what a map of non-manage
 d colonies\, checked in on year after year\, gives us. SaveTheHives.org\n
 \nImage: no asset fits the Oahu-specific angle well — flagged for Ronnie
 . Fallback: images/facebook/oak-tree-colony_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also 
 _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg)\, last used Aug 26 — general wild-colony-sur
 viving-on-its-own imagery\, reasonable reuse at a ~12-day gap.\n\nAlt text
 : "A honey bee colony's entrance in a cavity of an old oak tree." (if oak-
 tree fallback used)\n\nLink: keep the trailing "SaveTheHives.org" as plain
  text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\nSource:
  Neumann\, P. et al. (2026). Darwin's solution: honeybees survive mite vec
 tors and viruses through natural selection. Trends in Parasitology 42(8):3
 -5. https://www.cell.com/trends/parasitology/fulltext/S1471-4922(26)00156-
 X\n\nSuggested timing: Mon Sep 7\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: generated Aug 30 2
 026 run\, unchanged this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — The best time of day to go beelining (pra
 ctical)
DTSTART:20260909T150000Z
DTEND:20260909T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260906T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-besttimeofday-20260909@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot. Carr
 ied over unchanged from the Aug 30 2026 run.\n\nPost text:\nTiming matters
  almost as much as technique. The best window for beelining is mid-morning
  to early afternoon on a warm\, calm\, sunny day — bees are out foraging
  heavily\, wind isn't scattering their flight lines\, and you've got enoug
 h daylight left to follow more than one bee's route. Overcast\, windy\, or
  late-in-the-day trips are still possible\, just harder.\n\nEarly fall (ri
 ght now) is one of the best stretches of the whole year for it — plenty 
 of blooming forage still out\, and bees packing in stores before the weath
 er turns. Full how-to in the Learn tab. savethehives.org/?tab=learn 🐝\n
 \nImage: images/facebook/meadow-hero_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also availab
 le as _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg) — open wildflower meadow\, fits the mi
 d-day foraging framing.\n\nAlt text: "A honey bee foraging in a sunlit wil
 dflower meadow."\n\nLink: use savethehives.org/?tab=learn as trailing plai
 n text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\nSugges
 ted timing: Wed Sep 9\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: generated Aug 30 2026 run\, u
 nchanged this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — We're part of a bigger movement (communit
 y)
DTSTART:20260913T150000Z
DTEND:20260913T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260906T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-biggermovement-20260913@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot. Carried
  over unchanged from the Aug 30 2026 run. Pulled from the standing idea al
 ready flagged in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §6 ("worth a 'we're part of a b
 igger movement' post").\n\nPost text:\nSaveTheHives isn't the only citizen
 -science project keeping an eye on honey bees right now — and that's a g
 ood thing. Auburn University and the Apiary Inspectors of America run an a
 nnual national beekeeping survey tracking managed-colony health across the
  country. Different focus (they track managed hives\, we track non-managed
  ones)\, same underlying idea: real data comes from real people paying att
 ention\, not just from labs.\n\nThe more of these efforts there are\, the 
 fuller the picture gets. If you've logged a hive with us\, you're part of 
 that bigger picture too. SaveTheHives.org\n\nImage: images/facebook/map-sc
 reenshot-full_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also available as _fb-portrait-1080
 x1350.jpg) — full\, uncropped map screenshot\, unused in any post before
  Sep 13.\n\nAlt text: "Screenshot of the full SaveTheHives map showing hiv
 e pin locations across the region."\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as plai
 n text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\nSugges
 ted timing: Sun Sep 13\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: generated Aug 30 2026 run\, 
 unchanged this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Two paths to the same trait (research hoo
 k)
DTSTART:20260914T150000Z
DTEND:20260914T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260906T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-hygienicselection-20260914@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot — fresh res
 earch-hook search this run (Sep 6 2026)\, per FACEBOOK_POST_LOG.md's "Next
  up" note to resume searching starting Sep 14.\n\nExpert-authorship check:
  NO DIRECT MATCH. Amiri\, E.\, Mehrparvar\, S.\, Valizadeh\, B.\, Wagoner
 \, K. (2026). "Honey bee hygienic selection impacts virus dynamics of both
  bees and Varroa mites." Virus Research 367:199715 (published online Mar 1
 8\, 2026). Lead author Esmaeil Amiri and co-authors Somayeh Mehrparvar and
  Bita Valizadeh are all Mississippi State University\; Kaira Wagoner is UN
 C Greensboro. None of the four are listed by name in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.m
 d. However: Amiri works in the same Mississippi State apiculture program a
 s Jeff Harris (already in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md's Batch 1 outreach list\,
  not yet contacted\, the researcher who originally developed the VSH-bred 
 bee lines this study builds on) — a collegial/institutional connection\,
  not a co-authorship match\, so the post copy below does not name-drop Har
 ris. Separately\, worth flagging for Ronnie: Dr. Kaira Wagoner (UNC Greens
 boro\, Plant and Pollinator Center) is an active Varroa/hygienic-behavior 
 researcher based in North Carolina — same state as Tarpy/NC State — an
 d is not currently in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md. Suggested addition\, not mad
 e automatically\; worth considering as an in-state lead.\n\nPost text:\nMa
 naged beekeeping is trying to breed something wild colonies may already ha
 ve for free. A new study out of Mississippi State and UNC Greensboro (Viru
 s Research\, Mar 2026) found that honey bee colonies selected for "hygieni
 c behavior" — the instinct to detect and remove mite-infested brood befo
 re it hatches — don't just carry fewer Varroa mites. The viruses those m
 ites spread show up at lower levels too\, in both the bees and the mites t
 hemselves.\n\nThat's the exact trait breeders are trying to engineer throu
 gh years of selective breeding programs. Non-managed colonies that survive
  winter after winter may already carry it naturally — no breeding progra
 m required. Two paths\, same destination. Every colony we map is a chance 
 to find out which one got there on its own. SaveTheHives.org\n\nImage: no 
 dedicated asset for lab/breeding research — fallback to images/facebook/
 honeybee-on-comb_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg)
 \, last used Aug 31\, ~2-week gap\, reasonable reuse.\n\nAlt text: "Close-
 up of a honey bee on honeycomb."\n\nLink: keep the trailing "SaveTheHives.
 org" as plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generat
 e.\n\nSource: Amiri\, E.\, Mehrparvar\, S.\, Valizadeh\, B.\, Wagoner\, K.
  (2026). Honey bee hygienic selection impacts virus dynamics of both bees 
 and Varroa mites. Virus Research 367:199715. DOI: 10.1016/j.virusres.2026.
 199715\n\nSuggested timing: Mon Sep 14\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new content 
 this run — uses the "VSH breeding programs are trying to engineer what w
 ild survivors may already have" framing already suggested in FACEBOOK_STAR
 TER_PACK.md §6 ("two paths to the same goal").
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Use the landscape\, not just the compass 
 (practical)
DTSTART:20260916T150000Z
DTEND:20260916T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260906T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-landscapenavigation-20260916@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot — n
 ew content this run (Sep 6 2026)\, no ready post existed for this slot. De
 liberately a different angle from the Aug 26 "What you're actually looking
  for" post (bee-ID/what-a-colony-looks-like) and the Jul 29 "Triangulation
 " post (crossing two flight lines) — this one is about holding your bear
 ing over a long walk using natural landmarks.\n\nPost text:\nA compass get
 s you pointed the right direction\, but the landscape does a lot of the wo
 rk too. Once you've got a bee's flight line\, look for a streambed\, fence
  line\, or ridge that roughly follows it — natural landmarks make it muc
 h easier to hold your bearing over a long walk\, especially if tree cover 
 blocks your view of any fixed point ahead.\n\nIt's a small trick\, but it'
 s the difference between wandering in the general direction and walking st
 raight to the tree. Full field guide in the Learn tab. savethehives.org/?t
 ab=learn 🐝\n\nImage: images/facebook/learn-terrain-b_fb-square-1080x108
 0.jpg (also _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg) — unused in any post so far\, sh
 ows a bee flying along a winding stream through open grassland\, a strong 
 visual match for this specific tip.\n\nAlt text: "Illustration of a honey 
 bee flying along a winding stream through open grassland\, from the SaveTh
 eHives Learn tab terrain guide."\n\nLink: use savethehives.org/?tab=learn 
 as trailing plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will gen
 erate.\n\nSuggested timing: Wed Sep 16\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new content 
 this run — first use of the learn-terrain-b asset in a post.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — This started in North Carolina. It didn't
  stay there. (community)
DTSTART:20260920T150000Z
DTEND:20260920T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260906T220000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-startedinnc-20260920@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot — new 
 content this run (Sep 6 2026)\, no ready post existed for this slot.\n\nPo
 st text:\nThe original 1\,150+ legacy records behind this map started arou
 nd Raleigh\, NC — but by the time the first version of this project went
  dark around 2015\, entries had come in from all over\, not just the Trian
 gle. Wild honey bee colonies don't respect state lines\, and neither does 
 this map.\n\nWherever you're reading this from\, there's a real chance a c
 olony worth logging is closer than you think. SaveTheHives.org 🐝\n\nIma
 ge: images/facebook/oak-tree-colony_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also _fb-port
 rait-1080x1350.jpg) — reused from the Sep 7 fallback slot\, ~13-day gap
 \, flagged for Ronnie as a placeholder. Also considered images/teaser-bee-
 poster.jpg (the landing page's animated-bee teaser frame\, unused in any p
 ost so far — same subject\, a bee at a hollow-tree entrance) but it's on
 ly 17KB\, likely too low-resolution for a full Facebook photo post\; verif
 y before using it in place of the oak-tree fallback.\n\nAlt text: "A honey
  bee colony's entrance in a cavity of an old oak tree."\n\nLink: keep the 
 trailing URL as plain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will
  generate.\n\nSuggested timing: Sun Sep 20\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new cont
 ent this run — behind-the-scenes/origin angle\, distinct from the Jul 22
  "How this started" post (focused on the Tarpy friendship/Feral Bee Projec
 t origin) — this one leans on the geographic-reach detail specifically\,
  as a soft call for participation beyond NC.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
